Celebrating my 800th post with this cool problem.

<blockquote>Let  $ABC$  be a triangle. A point  $P$  is chosen inside  $\triangle ABC$  such that  $\angle BPC+\angle BAC=180^{\circ}$ . The lines  $AP,BP,CP$  intersect  $BC,CA,AB$  at  $P_A,P_B,P_C$  respectively. Let  $X_A$  be the second intersection of the circumcircles of  $\triangle ABC$  and  $\triangle AP_BP_C$  . Similarly define  $X_B,X_C$ . Let  $B'$  be the intersection of lines  $AX_A,CX_C$ , and let  $C$  be the intersection of lines  $AX_A,BX_B$ . Prove that lines  $BB'$  and  $CC'$  intersect on the circumcircle of  $\triangle AP_BP_C$ .</blockquote>
[asy]
