First, for each empty square  $S$ , we shall investigate the neighboring squares of  $S$ . This leads to the following claim.**Claim.** The neighboring squares of each empty square  $S$  has the following form, rotated or reflected:
[asy]
size(4cm);
for (int i=1; i<3; ++i) {
draw((0,i)--(3,i), grey);
draw((i,0)--(i,3), grey);
}
draw(box((0,0),(3,3)), black);
path vdomino = box((0.1,0.1), (0.9,1.9));
path hdomino = box((0.1,0.1), (1.9,0.9));
filldraw(shift(0,0)*vdomino, lightcyan, purple+2.3);
filldraw(shift(2,1)*vdomino, lightcyan, purple+2.3);
filldraw(shift(1,0)*hdomino, lightcyan, purple+2.3);
filldraw(shift(0,2)*hdomino, lightcyan, purple+2.3);
label(" $S$ ", (1.5, 1.5));
[/asy]**Proof.** First, note that  $S$  is not on the boundary of the grid. Indeed, if  $S$  is on the WLOG, bottom row of the grid, then the squares to the left and right of  $S$  (if they exist) must be covered with a vertical domino. But then, the square on the top of  $S$  can neither be blank due to (ii) nor be tiled with a domino due to (iii), a contradiction. Hence,  $S$  is not on the boundary and by the same argument, the vertical dominoes covering the left and right of  $S$  span different rows. Then, since top and bottom squares of  $S$  must be covered by a horizontal domino due to (ii) and (iii). Hence, the claim follows.  $\blacksquare$ Next, we will construct a one-to-four mapping from the set of empty squares to the occupied squares. This can be done in the form of a double counting argument as follows. Let there be  $k$  empty squares and  $d$  dominoes. Call a domino  $D$  and a square  $S$  to be *adjacent* if  $S$  shares one of its sides with a part of the longer side of  $D$  (hence union of  $D$  and  $S$  is an  $L$ -shape). We shall count the number of pairs  $(D, S)$  where domino  $d$  and empty square  $S$  are adjacent. Counting from the perspective of empty squares, due to our claim, this number is  $4k$ . Counting from the perspective of dominoes, this number is at most  $2d$  since each domino is adjacent to at most  $2$  empty squares. This gives us the following inequality:  $$ 4k\le2d \implies 2k\le \frac{mn-k}{2} \implies k\le\frac{mn}{5}. $$  However, this inequality is strict, since the domino covering the corner square of the grid has at most one empty square adjacent to it. So, we are done.  $\square$ This style of argument (and also the problem) is quite similar to Evan's solution to [url = https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h1869831p12679987]Singapore Open MO 2019/5[/url].

