<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $X$  be the foot of the perpendicular from  $L$  to  $\overline{AB}.$  Also, without loss of generality, let the side length of the hexagon be  $2\sqrt{3}.$  (This doesn't lose generality because we can scale the hexagon up or down so that its side length is  $2\sqrt{3}$ .) Note that   $$ AX=XB=\sqrt{3}. $$  Note that  $\triangle{ABC}$  is isosceles and  $\angle{ABC}=120^\circ,$  so it follows that   $$ \angle{LAB}=30^\circ. $$   Similarly, we have   $$ \angle{LBA=30^\circ}. $$  Thus,  $\triangle{LAX}$  and  $\triangle{LBX}$  are both  $30-60-90$  triangles, so we have
\begin{align*}
LX &= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{3}} 
&= 1.
\end{align*}
Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
[ALB] &= \frac{LX \cdot AB}{2} 
&= \frac{1\cdot2\sqrt{3}}{2} 
&= \frac{2\sqrt{3}}{2} 
&= \sqrt{3}.
\end{align*}
On the other hand, note that
\begin{align*}
[ABCDEF] &= 6 \cdot \frac{(2\sqrt{3})^2\cdot\sqrt{3}}{4} 
&= \frac{3}{2} \cdot 12\sqrt{3} 
&= 18\sqrt{3},
\end{align*}
so the answer is
\begin{align*}
\frac{[ALB]}{[ABCDEF]} &= \frac{\sqrt{3}}{18\sqrt{3}} 
&= \boxed{\frac{1}{18}}.
\end{align*} $\square$</details>

